1. The SA-SAMS Integration Reality
The hard truth: SA-SAMS is an old Microsoft Access (.mdb) desktop application. It does not have a modern REST API. 
Your integration options are:
Table
Approach	How it works	Difficulty	Reliability
A. Desktop Sync Agent	Small Windows app installed on the school's SA-SAMS computer. Reads the .mdb file directly from C:\EDUSOLSAMS\Data\ and syncs learner/subject data to your cloud DB.	Medium	High
B. CSV Import/Export	School exports CSVs from SA-SAMS (learner lists, subjects). Your system imports them. Marks go back via CSV into SA-SAMS at term-end.	Low	Medium
C. Bi-directional .mdb write	Your agent writes marks/attendance directly into the SA-SAMS .mdb file.	High	Risky — can corrupt DB
My recommendation: Start with A + B hybrid.
Build a lightweight Windows tray app that reads the SA-SAMS .mdb to pull learner names, grades, classes, and subject allocations automatically.
For marks export back to SA-SAMS, use the validated CSV method (schools already know this workflow). 
Over time, add one-click CSV generation formatted exactly for SA-SAMS import.
Why this wins: Competitors like d6 and Staffroom do manual export/import.  If you can make the initial data pull automatic (reading the .mdb), you eliminate the biggest setup friction.
2. Your Killer Feature: "Project Panic Prevention"
This is your moat. Position it explicitly:
"No more 9pm Sunday night project discoveries."
How it works:
Teacher logs into your web app and creates an upcoming project: "Grade 5: Solar System Model — due 15 August"
Your system automatically:
Shows it on the parent dashboard
Sends a WhatsApp message to parents: "📚 New project alert: Solar System Model for Natural Sciences. Due: 15 Aug. Posted 3 weeks early — no surprises!"
Sends reminder WhatsApps at 2 weeks, 1 week, and 3 days before due date
Parent can mark "We've started" or upload a photo of work-in-progress (optional engagement feature)
Why primary schools specifically:
Younger kids don't self-manage. Parents are the project managers.
Primary school teachers are more willing to try new tools than high school teachers (who are locked into rigid exam schedules).
The emotional value is highest here — parents of Grade 1–7 kids feel the panic most acutely.
3. MVP Feature Set (Build in 8–10 Weeks)
Table
Module	Features
SA-SAMS Sync	Windows agent reads .mdb → auto-imports learners, grades, classes, subjects. One-time setup.
Project Board	Teachers post upcoming projects/tests with due dates, subject, grade, brief description, and material list.
